**READ THE TWO CYCLE RISES HONESTLY.** At photo_cid p2 and at screen_terminal
p6 the candidate executes FEWER instructions, FEWER branches and FEWER
mispredicts and spends MORE cycles. Nothing this change does can produce that;
it is code placement — the landed chunks shrink `encode_block_syntax` by ~46 %
of its instructions and dissolve the `normalize` symbol, so everything after
them in `.text` moves. The size of the effect is bounded by what the entropy
path IS at p2: **0.08 % of that cell's instructions**, which is all preset 2
could ever gain or lose here on merit. The same signature was measured for
chunk A alone (p2 instructions -0.017 %, branches -0.02 %, mispredicts -0.04 %,
cycles +0.38 %) before B and F existed, so it is not specific to one chunk.

=================== WALL CLOCK, AND THE CONTROL THAT MAKES IT READABLE ===================

`tools/perf_ab.sh`, 21 interleaved paired rounds per cell, order randomised per
round, neither side niced, every row byte-identical. `speedup = base/cand`, so
> 1 means the candidate is faster.

  cell                       speedup   ratio    p25      p75
  CONTROL photo_cid p2        0.997    1.0026   0.9989   1.0072   <- SAME BINARY
  CONTROL photo_cid p6        0.995    1.0048   1.0012   1.0080   <- SAME BINARY
  photo_cid 512² p6         **1.037**  0.9642   0.9593   0.9695
  photo_clic 512² p6        **1.029**  0.9715   0.9654   0.9724
  gradient 512 p6           **1.012**  0.9886   0.9742   1.0018
  screen_terminal 512² p6     1.004    0.9958   0.9817   1.0060
  gradient 256 p6             1.001    0.9989   0.9791   1.0075
  photo_cid 512² p2           0.996    1.0039   0.9981   1.0090
  photo_clic 512² p2          0.995    1.0051   1.0015   1.0063
  gradient 512 p2             0.997    1.0029   0.9980   1.0055
  gradient 256 p2             0.996    1.0041   0.9989   1.0069
  screen_terminal 512² p2     0.994    1.0062   1.0044   1.0094

**RUN THE CONTROL. THIS IS THE FINDING WORTH CARRYING PAST THIS CHUNK.** The
first two rows are `perf_ab.sh` comparing `perf_encode.base` against a BYTE
COPY of itself. It reports the candidate slot **0.3 % slower at p2 and 0.5 %
slower at p6, with the p6 quartile span entirely above 1.0.** Whatever the
mechanism (this record does not claim one — the box had a sibling lane on it
throughout, and no attempt was made to separate harness bias from contention),
the consequence is arithmetic: **a sub-1 % wall-clock "regression" from this
harness under these conditions is not evidence of a regression.**

With that floor in hand the table reads cleanly. Every p2 row sits at
1.0029-1.0062 against a p2 control of 1.0026 — nulls, and the hardware counters
say the same thing from the other side (p2 instructions -0.08 %). The two photo
p6 rows sit at 0.9642 / 0.9715 against a p6 control of 1.0048, with
non-overlapping quartiles — a real win of roughly 3-4 % once the floor is
subtracted, which is the same size the pinned cycle counters give (-2.96 %).

Earlier in the day the same grid was run for chunk A ALONE, before B and F
existed and before any control: photo_cid p6 1.021x, photo_clic p6 1.017x,
everything else within +-0.8 % with every p2 row on the slow side. Those p2 rows
are what prompted the control; they are recorded in the TSV rather than dropped,
because "chunk A looked like a uniform p2 regression until a same-binary run
showed the same sign" is the useful part.

