=================== WHAT IS AND IS NOT ESTABLISHED ===================

ESTABLISHED, at this commit on this host: every callgrind Ir/Bc/Bcm total in the
table above (deterministic, and each build's byte-identity re-checked from its
own instrumented run); the per-chunk Ir deltas that table implies; the hardware
instruction/cycle/branch-miss/IPC deltas at three cells; the twelve wall-clock
rows and the two control rows; the C `file:line` for every structural claim.

NOT MEASURED, and not to be inferred: aarch64 performance (the gates ran there,
no timing did); 10-bit; inter frames; presets other than 2 and 6; qp other than
40; aarch64 memory and peak HEAP anywhere (the MEMORY section gives x86 RSS at
two 2048 arms and says what it does not cover); the MECHANISM of either the p2/
screenshot cycle rises or the base-vs-base harness bias (both are reported as
what they are, an unattributable placement effect and an unattributed floor);
whether a quiet box would show a different control (the sibling lane was live
for every wall-clock row, including the control, which is what makes the two
comparable to each other).

=================== MEMORY ===================

**Peak RSS, x86-64, gradient 2048x2048 qp 40 preset 13, `/usr/bin/time -v`,
5 repetitions, KiB:**

  arm     base                             candF                          verdict
  still   68,920 (68,796-69,156)           68,824 (68,624-69,264)         NULL
  inter  109,296 (109,216-109,668)        109,568 (108,916-109,968)       NULL

Both pairs' min/max ranges OVERLAP COMPLETELY, so both medians (-0.14 % and
+0.25 %) are inside the run-to-run spread. The inter cell's `.obu` is
byte-identical between the two binaries at 139,761 B — a byte-identity data
point on the 2048 inter path as well as a memory one.

That is the expected result and the structural reason is worth stating so the
next reader does not have to re-measure: **the landed diff adds, removes and
resizes NO allocation.** `pipeline.rs` replaces a loop with a helper;
`coeff_c.rs` peels an iteration; `range_coder.rs` moves the SAME
`Vec::resize` call from `normalize`'s body into the `flush` it split out of it,
on the same path with the same argument. `TxbScratch` is untouched — chunk E,
the only change that would have moved a buffer, was reverted. RSS can still move
on code size and page mapping, which is what the two overlapping ranges are.

**aarch64 RSS was NOT measured** (the aarch64 box ran the byte gates, not
timing or memory), so no aarch64 2048-inter figure is claimed. Peak HEAP
(heaptrack) was not measured either; these are RSS numbers and are labelled as
such.

=================== THE QUIET-BOX RE-RUN, WHICH SETTLES BOTH QUESTIONS ===================

The section above left two things open: whether the harness bias is intrinsic or
contention-induced, and whether the p2 rows would look different without a
sibling lane on the box. Both were re-measured after the gate chains finished,
on a box at load 0.6-1.1 with nothing else running (`uptime` and `ps` captured
before and after, in the run log), same binaries, same 21 interleaved paired
rounds.

  pair                        cell           ratio    speedup   p25      p75
  CONTROL base vs base        photo_cid p2   1.0052   0.995     0.9974   1.0083
  CONTROL base vs base        photo_cid p6   1.0043   0.996     0.9992   1.0062
  base vs candF               photo_cid p2   1.0046   0.995     1.0009   1.0082
  base vs candF               photo_cid p6   **0.9660**  **1.035**  0.9604   0.9690

**THE BIAS IS NOT CONTENTION.** The quiet control reads 1.0052 / 1.0043 against
the contended control's 1.0026 / 1.0048 — the same 0.3-0.5 % against the
candidate slot either way. So `perf_ab.sh` under-reports the candidate by that
much as a property of the harness, not of the box, and every ratio this campaign
has ever recorded carries it. (This record does NOT claim a mechanism; it claims
the measurement, twice, under conditions that differ in the obvious suspect.)

**AND THE p2 "REGRESSION" IS THE BIAS, EXACTLY.** On a quiet box the candidate
reads 1.0046 at p2 where the SAME BINARY reads 1.0052 — the candidate is, if
anything, marginally faster than itself, and the two quartile spans lie on top of
each other. There is no p2 regression to explain. What is left is the p6 row at
0.9660 against a 1.0043 control: **3.8 % apart with non-overlapping quartiles**,
i.e. a real ~3.9 % win once the floor is subtracted, which is the same size the
pinned cycle counters gave (-2.96 %) plus the floor.

The p2 CYCLE counter rise (+0.82 %, taken pinned, both sides, with every
instruction/branch/mispredict count falling) is a separate observation and still
stands as an unattributed code-placement effect; it just no longer has a
wall-clock consequence anyone can see.
